<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Remaining keys are generated from the INFOPLIST_KEY_* build settings
	  (GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE stays on and merges into this file). This file holds the
	  keys that cannot be expressed as a build setting, or that are easier to read with
	  their reasoning beside them.
	-->

	<!--
	  Export compliance. Without this key App Store Connect asks the export questions on
	  every upload and holds the build at "Missing Compliance" until they are answered,
	  which blocks repeated TestFlight builds.

	  ⚠️ THE VALUE BELOW IS A LEGAL DECLARATION AND NEEDS THE OWNER'S SIGN-OFF.

	  The case for `false`: the app implements no cryptography of its own. Data at rest
	  uses CryptoKit AES-GCM (an OS-provided implementation) and network traffic uses
	  TLS. Both are the standard exempt cases.

	  The complication, which the owner should weigh before the first upload: the
	  dependency graph pins swift-nio-ssl 2.36.1, which vendors BoringSSL, reached
	  through the AWS SDK's HTTP client. So the binary can ship a third-party TLS
	  implementation rather than only the system one. That is ordinary mass-market TLS
	  and is normally still exempt, but it is a self-classification call to make
	  knowingly rather than by default. Removing Bedrock (review decision 5) would
	  remove the question with it.
	-->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>

	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<!--
		  From iOS 17 on, ATS blocks cleartext connections to IP addresses by default.
		  Self-hosted OpenAI-compatible inference servers (Ollama, LM Studio, vLLM,
		  LiteLLM) normally run on the LAN over plain HTTP with no certificate, so
		  without this the local-server feature cannot connect at all.

		  This is the narrow exception: it permits cleartext only to private and
		  link-local IP ranges, .local domains, and unqualified host names. Public
		  hosts still require HTTPS, and OpenAICompatibleEndpointValidator enforces
		  the same rule in the app before a request is ever built.
		-->
		<key>NSAllowsLocalNetworking</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>
</dict>
</plist>
